17
00:03:17,980 --> 00:03:20,230
For those of you that just
came in, feel free to vote.

18
00:03:20,230 --> 00:03:23,220
The first one is that
importance sampling does not

19
00:03:23,220 --> 00:03:24,870
leverage the Markov assumption.

20
00:03:24,870 --> 00:03:25,730
Do not need this.

21
00:03:25,730 --> 00:03:28,170
So this is false.

22
00:03:28,170 --> 00:03:31,262
Important sampling can work
with non-Markov systems, which

23
00:03:31,262 --> 00:03:32,470
is one of the benefits of it.

24
00:03:32,470 --> 00:03:35,370
It makes very little assumptions
over the data distribution

25
00:03:35,370 --> 00:03:37,030
generating process.

26
00:03:37,030 --> 00:03:39,240
So it can be used in
just similar to how

27
00:03:39,240 --> 00:03:41,610
we could use Monte Carlo
methods to estimate

28
00:03:41,610 --> 00:03:43,630
the value of a policy
through rollouts.

29
00:03:43,630 --> 00:03:45,930
This also is very general.

30
00:03:45,930 --> 00:03:48,750
Let's go through the
next one as well.

31
00:03:48,750 --> 00:03:51,480
So let's now use the
Markov assumption.

32
00:03:51,480 --> 00:03:57,128
For this one, the
first one is true.

33
00:03:57,128 --> 00:03:59,670
So we can think of using the
advantage function of one policy

34
00:03:59,670 --> 00:04:01,530
and samples from the other.

35
00:04:01,530 --> 00:04:04,530
The second is that
we can importance

36
00:04:04,530 --> 00:04:06,540
weight between the
two policies and get

37
00:04:06,540 --> 00:04:08,670
the samples from policy one.

38
00:04:08,670 --> 00:04:11,350
So it's not really
an exact bound,

39
00:04:11,350 --> 00:04:14,050
but it turns out we can
bound how off that is.

40
00:04:14,050 --> 00:04:16,060
And the reason it's not
exact is because we're

41
00:04:16,060 --> 00:04:18,950
using samples of
states from one policy,

42
00:04:18,950 --> 00:04:21,519
whereas in reality, the
other policy might visit

43
00:04:21,519 --> 00:04:24,550
different types of states.

44
00:04:24,550 --> 00:04:27,000
And PPO uses these
types of ideas.

45
00:04:30,420 --> 00:04:32,717
And the approximation
error is bounded

46
00:04:32,717 --> 00:04:34,300
by the average over
the states visited

47
00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:37,310
by one policy between
the two policies.

48
00:04:37,310 --> 00:04:40,330
So this is trying
to say, how bad is

49
00:04:40,330 --> 00:04:45,110
this approximation when we use
just samples from one policy,

50
00:04:45,110 --> 00:04:45,820
OK?

51
00:04:45,820 --> 00:04:49,042
That was one of the really nice
insights of that prior work,

52
00:04:49,042 --> 00:04:50,500
is to show you
actually could bound

53
00:04:50,500 --> 00:04:52,960
what is the error in the
app-- the approximation error

54
00:04:52,960 --> 00:04:55,510
that we induce by
pretending that we'd

55
00:04:55,510 --> 00:04:57,370
get to the same states
under policy two

56
00:04:57,370 --> 00:04:58,580
compared to policy one.

57
00:05:01,310 --> 00:05:01,810
Awesome.

58
00:05:01,810 --> 00:05:04,840
So last time we talked a bit
about learning from prior data.

59
00:05:04,840 --> 00:05:06,380
And really, the
last few lectures,

60
00:05:06,380 --> 00:05:08,980
we've been talking about how
to learn from human feedback

61
00:05:08,980 --> 00:05:11,170
or from past
demonstrations of people

62
00:05:11,170 --> 00:05:13,463
or historical data we have.

63
00:05:13,463 --> 00:05:15,130
And now we're going
to switch, and we're

64
00:05:15,130 --> 00:05:17,463
going to think more about,
well, what if we can actually

65
00:05:17,463 --> 00:05:18,357
gather that data?

66
00:05:18,357 --> 00:05:20,690
And of course, that's where
we started at the beginning.

67
00:05:20,690 --> 00:05:22,850
We thought about-- if
we are quite early on,

68
00:05:22,850 --> 00:05:25,190
we thought about how
to evaluate policies,

69
00:05:25,190 --> 00:05:27,440
if we could gather data.

70
00:05:27,440 --> 00:05:30,585
But we didn't think a lot about
how that data was gathered.

71
00:05:30,585 --> 00:05:32,710
We talked about epsilon-greedy,
and we'll talk more

72
00:05:32,710 --> 00:05:34,510
about epsilon-greedy
today, but we

73
00:05:34,510 --> 00:05:38,920
didn't think super strategically
over the influence of the way we

74
00:05:38,920 --> 00:05:40,270
were gathering data.

75
00:05:40,270 --> 00:05:41,660
And so for the
next few lectures,

76
00:05:41,660 --> 00:05:43,262
we're going to talk
about that a lot.

77
00:05:43,262 --> 00:05:45,220
And that's really a
critical part, particularly

78
00:05:45,220 --> 00:05:46,645
for online
reinforcement learning.

79
00:05:46,645 --> 00:05:48,520
It's like, how do we
actually gather the data

80
00:05:48,520 --> 00:05:50,925
we need in order to learn
to make good decisions?

81
00:05:50,925 --> 00:05:53,550
And can we do this-- are there
better or worse ways to do this?

82
00:05:56,065 --> 00:05:58,690
So one of the things I want to
emphasize when we start thinking

83
00:05:58,690 --> 00:06:01,210
about this part
of the course is,

84
00:06:01,210 --> 00:06:03,182
a lot of reinforcement
learning, particularly

85
00:06:03,182 --> 00:06:04,640
if you have simulated
environments,

86
00:06:04,640 --> 00:06:07,480
focuses on computational
efficiency.

87
00:06:07,480 --> 00:06:10,450
So we think about
things like any place

88
00:06:10,450 --> 00:06:11,720
where you have a simulator.

89
00:06:11,720 --> 00:06:17,080
So if we want to do Atari
or if we want to do MuJoCo,

90
00:06:17,080 --> 00:06:19,720
in these cases,
computational time

91
00:06:19,720 --> 00:06:23,440
is essentially the same as
data, because you can either

92
00:06:23,440 --> 00:06:25,510
be using that additional
computational time

93
00:06:25,510 --> 00:06:28,330
to sample from your
simulator or to actually

94
00:06:28,330 --> 00:06:31,820
spend more time computing
a Q function or policy.

95
00:06:31,820 --> 00:06:35,200
And so to some
extent, simulators

96
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:38,530
blend the difference between
computational efficiency

97
00:06:38,530 --> 00:06:42,072
and data efficiency because
it's all just computation.

98
00:06:42,072 --> 00:06:44,530
Like, you have a simulator and
you can either give you data

99
00:06:44,530 --> 00:06:46,615
or you can use it to
do Bellman backups

100
00:06:46,615 --> 00:06:48,490
or whatever else you
want, but you could just

101
00:06:48,490 --> 00:06:51,280
count how much total resources
you're using essentially

102
00:06:51,280 --> 00:06:52,930
in terms of computation.

103
00:06:52,930 --> 00:06:55,610
There are a lot of
other domains where

104
00:06:55,610 --> 00:06:58,400
computation is really
separate from samples, like,

105
00:06:58,400 --> 00:06:59,340
from actual data.

106
00:06:59,340 --> 00:07:01,457
So this is data.

107
00:07:01,457 --> 00:07:03,290
And these are a lot of
the application areas

108
00:07:03,290 --> 00:07:05,420
that I tend to think about
and a number of other people

109
00:07:05,420 --> 00:07:06,253
think about as well.

110
00:07:06,253 --> 00:07:11,630
So if you think about something
like using mobile phones

111
00:07:11,630 --> 00:07:17,130
for health
interventions, or if you

112
00:07:17,130 --> 00:07:20,190
think about consumer
marketing, like,

113
00:07:20,190 --> 00:07:23,340
which ad to show
to people, or you

114
00:07:23,340 --> 00:07:29,100
think about
educational technology,

115
00:07:29,100 --> 00:07:31,010
or you think about climate.

116
00:07:33,670 --> 00:07:40,950
I'll do environmental policies.

117
00:07:40,950 --> 00:07:43,475
In all of these cases,
there's a real world

118
00:07:43,475 --> 00:07:44,600
that's happening out there.

119
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:46,710
There's a real students
or there's real patients

120
00:07:46,710 --> 00:07:48,000
or there's--

121
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:51,130
where you're trying
to decide, say,

122
00:07:51,130 --> 00:07:55,050
policies to encourage wildlife
conservation or others.

123
00:07:55,050 --> 00:07:58,720
And so you have computers you
can use to compute that policy,

124
00:07:58,720 --> 00:08:00,150
and then you have
real world data.

125
00:08:00,150 --> 00:08:03,210
And the real world data
I'll call samples or sample

126
00:08:03,210 --> 00:08:05,370
efficiency here,
and you care often

127
00:08:05,370 --> 00:08:08,490
a lot about that real world
data and ho-- squeezing the most

128
00:08:08,490 --> 00:08:09,650
you can out of it.

129
00:08:09,650 --> 00:08:12,640
So in particular, you might
imagine that if you have, say,

130
00:08:12,640 --> 00:08:16,330
data from 500,000 patients
or something like that,

131
00:08:16,330 --> 00:08:19,062
that's quite a lot, bless you,
but it's not nearly as large

132
00:08:19,062 --> 00:08:21,520
as what you would normally have
in the case of, say, Atari,

133
00:08:21,520 --> 00:08:24,000
where you can just run
the simulator forever.

134
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:25,870
Or in the case of
things like AlphaGo,

135
00:08:25,870 --> 00:08:28,780
where again, you could
just play the board game,

136
00:08:28,780 --> 00:08:31,800
go against each other-- against
simulated agents forever.

137
00:08:31,800 --> 00:08:33,720
So a lot of the
things we're going

138
00:08:33,720 --> 00:08:34,890
to be talking about over
the next few lectures

139
00:08:34,890 --> 00:08:37,409
are just going to assume that
we care about this because we

140
00:08:37,409 --> 00:08:38,774
can't get infinite data.

141
00:08:38,774 --> 00:08:40,899
So we have to be-- we're
thinking about cases where

142
00:08:40,899 --> 00:08:42,100
like, these are
coming from patients

143
00:08:42,100 --> 00:08:43,549
or they're coming from students.

144
00:08:43,549 --> 00:08:45,850
And so we want to be much
more careful with the data

145
00:08:45,850 --> 00:08:46,840
we're gathering.

146
00:08:46,840 --> 00:08:48,850
And think about how
we could maximize

147
00:08:48,850 --> 00:08:50,590
the information we
get out of those

148
00:08:50,590 --> 00:08:53,470
to try to make good decisions.

149
00:08:53,470 --> 00:08:55,810
So when we start
to do that, there's

150
00:08:55,810 --> 00:08:58,390
a number of different things
we want to consider in terms

151
00:08:58,390 --> 00:09:00,670
of how good are the
different algorithms we're

152
00:09:00,670 --> 00:09:02,320
going to consider?

153
00:09:02,320 --> 00:09:07,120
So one thing might be that
if it converges at all--

154
00:09:07,120 --> 00:09:10,310
and we've seen before that
from the deadly triad,

155
00:09:10,310 --> 00:09:12,440
we're not always
guaranteed to converge.

156
00:09:12,440 --> 00:09:19,270
So we've seen that
for some settings

157
00:09:19,270 --> 00:09:24,312
where this is not guaranteed
or it hasn't been proven yet.

158
00:09:24,312 --> 00:09:26,020
So you're not even
necessarily guaranteed

159
00:09:26,020 --> 00:09:27,250
to converge to anything.

160
00:09:27,250 --> 00:09:28,058
It might chatter.

161
00:09:28,058 --> 00:09:28,850
It might oscillate.

162
00:09:28,850 --> 00:09:30,570
It might not go to
anything stable.

163
00:09:30,570 --> 00:09:32,320
Second question you
might ask is if you're

164
00:09:32,320 --> 00:09:35,268
going to be guaranteed to
converge to the optimal policy.

165
00:09:35,268 --> 00:09:37,060
And then a third thing
that might be really

166
00:09:37,060 --> 00:09:38,540
important is, how quickly?

167
00:09:38,540 --> 00:09:40,665
So in this case, it's
going to be, how much data?

168
00:09:44,667 --> 00:09:46,750
And we're going to see
different types of measures

169
00:09:46,750 --> 00:09:48,583
to evaluate different
reinforcement learning

170
00:09:48,583 --> 00:09:49,280
algorithms.

171
00:09:49,280 --> 00:09:51,230
So let me just give
you an illustration too

172
00:09:51,230 --> 00:09:53,500
of why these things might
look quite different.

173
00:09:53,500 --> 00:09:55,060
So imagine that
you have something

174
00:09:55,060 --> 00:10:01,430
like this where this is time,
where this is a reward, OK?

175
00:10:01,430 --> 00:10:03,180
So you have really
different algorithms.

176
00:10:03,180 --> 00:10:04,638
You could have
algorithms that look

177
00:10:04,638 --> 00:10:09,410
like this, that might
be one algorithm,

178
00:10:09,410 --> 00:10:11,000
or you could have
an algorithm that

179
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:17,310
looks like this, really smooth,
and you could have algorithms

180
00:10:17,310 --> 00:10:20,440
that in general, maybe
most of the time do great,

181
00:10:20,440 --> 00:10:23,070
but periodically make
terrible mistakes.

182
00:10:23,070 --> 00:10:25,980
Versus could have
another algorithm

183
00:10:25,980 --> 00:10:29,850
which never does awesome,
but is always pretty good.

184
00:10:29,850 --> 00:10:31,900
And those are really
different types of behavior.

185
00:10:31,900 --> 00:10:35,560
So if you think about that in
terms of, say, an AI clinician,

186
00:10:35,560 --> 00:10:40,710
you could have an AI clinician
that on average helps,

187
00:10:40,710 --> 00:10:43,030
like, let's say, 80% of
your desired outcomes.

188
00:10:43,030 --> 00:10:44,905
Like, it helps you manage
your blood pressure

189
00:10:44,905 --> 00:10:47,280
with 80% accurate fidelity.

190
00:10:47,280 --> 00:10:50,122
Or it could be that for
80% of the population,

191
00:10:50,122 --> 00:10:52,330
it helps them completely
manage their blood pressure.

192
00:10:52,330 --> 00:10:54,770
And for 20% of them, it fails.

193
00:10:54,770 --> 00:10:57,850
So those are really different
types of performance guarantees,

194
00:10:57,850 --> 00:10:59,790
and we'll think about
whether trading off

195
00:10:59,790 --> 00:11:01,498
between those and what
sort of algorithms

196
00:11:01,498 --> 00:11:03,730
guarantee us to have different
sorts of performance.

197
00:11:07,290 --> 00:11:10,140
So we'll start to introduce
different types of settings

198
00:11:10,140 --> 00:11:12,490
and ways to evaluate the
quality of algorithms,

199
00:11:12,490 --> 00:11:14,320
and we're going to
start with bandits.

200
00:11:14,320 --> 00:11:16,800
And we've talked very
briefly about bandits

201
00:11:16,800 --> 00:11:20,170
in the context of ChatGPT
and preference learning.

202
00:11:20,170 --> 00:11:21,775
We'll talk a lot
more about them now,

203
00:11:21,775 --> 00:11:24,150
and then we'll move back into
the Markov decision process

204
00:11:24,150 --> 00:11:24,910
case.

205
00:11:24,910 --> 00:11:26,820
A lot of the ideas from
bandits will turn out

206
00:11:26,820 --> 00:11:30,330
to exactly or quite
easily translate over

207
00:11:30,330 --> 00:11:33,750
to the RL setting.

208
00:11:33,750 --> 00:11:34,700
OK.

209
00:11:34,700 --> 00:11:35,200
All right.

210
00:11:35,200 --> 00:11:36,310
So let's dive in.

211
00:11:36,310 --> 00:11:37,900
So what is a bandit?

212
00:11:37,900 --> 00:11:41,855
So a bandit is a really,
really simple RL problem.

213
00:11:41,855 --> 00:11:43,980
They've been studied since,
I think at least, like,

214
00:11:43,980 --> 00:11:45,250
around the 1920s.

215
00:11:45,250 --> 00:11:49,620
There's a very long history of
research on multi-armed bandits.

216
00:11:49,620 --> 00:11:52,420
It's been used for all
sorts of application areas.

217
00:11:52,420 --> 00:11:53,830
So let's describe what it is.

218
00:11:53,830 --> 00:11:57,300
So the idea in this case
is that there's no states.

219
00:11:57,300 --> 00:11:59,520
There's just a
finite set of arms.

220
00:11:59,520 --> 00:12:01,320
And arms are the
same as what we've

221
00:12:01,320 --> 00:12:02,830
been calling actions before.

222
00:12:02,830 --> 00:12:04,560
So as a concrete
example, you might

223
00:12:04,560 --> 00:12:07,390
think of there being
like 20 different ads you

224
00:12:07,390 --> 00:12:08,710
could show customers.

225
00:12:08,710 --> 00:12:11,200
And we're going to
assume that there's

226
00:12:11,200 --> 00:12:14,350
a probability distribution
over rewards for each arm.

227
00:12:14,350 --> 00:12:18,430
So maybe on average,
this gives you

228
00:12:18,430 --> 00:12:21,170
90% click through rate
for this particular ad,

229
00:12:21,170 --> 00:12:23,690
and this other ad gives
you 20% click through rate.

230
00:12:23,690 --> 00:12:25,100
But that's not known.

231
00:12:25,100 --> 00:12:26,568
That's not observed.

232
00:12:26,568 --> 00:12:28,360
And what will happen
is that each time step

233
00:12:28,360 --> 00:12:30,340
you get to select
one of the actions,

234
00:12:30,340 --> 00:12:33,100
and then the environment
will sample a reward

235
00:12:33,100 --> 00:12:35,680
from that stochastic variable.

236
00:12:35,680 --> 00:12:38,415
So if the click through rate
is 90% for that particular arm,

237
00:12:38,415 --> 00:12:39,790
most of the time,
you'll get a 1,

238
00:12:39,790 --> 00:12:41,800
and sometimes people
won't click on it.

239
00:12:41,800 --> 00:12:44,960
And the goal is to maximize
your cumulative reward.

240
00:12:44,960 --> 00:12:47,440
So overall time
steps that you get

241
00:12:47,440 --> 00:12:50,530
by most amount of, say, clicks.

242
00:12:50,530 --> 00:12:52,160
And this is a very
simple setting,

243
00:12:52,160 --> 00:12:54,260
but it's been used
extensively in a lot of areas.

244
00:12:54,260 --> 00:12:55,927
You could think about
this for something

245
00:12:55,927 --> 00:12:58,420
like, how could I-- if
I was doing something

246
00:12:58,420 --> 00:13:01,660
like a clinical trial, how might
I randomize the next person

247
00:13:01,660 --> 00:13:03,980
over what treatment to get,
a treatment or a control,

248
00:13:03,980 --> 00:13:07,570
for example, for ads, for
many, many different types

249
00:13:07,570 --> 00:13:10,000
of application areas?

250
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:11,405
So I'm going to go through--

251
00:13:11,405 --> 00:13:13,030
I'm going to have
some running examples

252
00:13:13,030 --> 00:13:14,290
for this part of the
course, and we're

253
00:13:14,290 --> 00:13:15,700
going to have a sort
of a silly one that's

254
00:13:15,700 --> 00:13:16,700
going to be illustrative.

255
00:13:16,700 --> 00:13:18,867
So let's imagine that we're
trying to treat patients

256
00:13:18,867 --> 00:13:19,972
with broken toes.

257
00:13:19,972 --> 00:13:21,680
This is nothing to do
with medical stuff,

258
00:13:21,680 --> 00:13:23,363
so this is not medical advice.

259
00:13:23,363 --> 00:13:26,030
Imagine you have three different
options-- you could do surgery.

260
00:13:26,030 --> 00:13:28,460
You could buddy tape the
broken toe to another toe,

261
00:13:28,460 --> 00:13:29,800
or you could do nothing.

262
00:13:29,800 --> 00:13:32,440
And your outcome measure
is a binary variable about

263
00:13:32,440 --> 00:13:35,410
whether or not that toe
is healed or not healed

264
00:13:35,410 --> 00:13:37,210
after six weeks.

265
00:13:37,210 --> 00:13:38,223
So that's our setting.

266
00:13:38,223 --> 00:13:39,140
We've got broken toes.

267
00:13:39,140 --> 00:13:40,690
We want to figure out,
what's the best strategy

268
00:13:40,690 --> 00:13:41,458
for healing them?

269
00:13:41,458 --> 00:13:43,250
And we're not going to
do a clinical trial.

270
00:13:43,250 --> 00:13:44,270
Instead, we're
just going to say,

271
00:13:44,270 --> 00:13:45,970
well, sometimes people come
in and they've broken toes,

272
00:13:45,970 --> 00:13:47,410
and I'm going to
try to figure out

273
00:13:47,410 --> 00:13:51,562
over time, which thing is best.

274
00:13:51,562 --> 00:13:53,730
All right.

275
00:13:53,730 --> 00:13:56,700
So in this case, we're
going to model it

276
00:13:56,700 --> 00:13:59,040
as a multi-armed
bandit with three arms.

277
00:13:59,040 --> 00:14:01,230
The arms are the--
and we're going

278
00:14:01,230 --> 00:14:03,150
to model each arm as
a Bernoulli variable

279
00:14:03,150 --> 00:14:05,310
with an unknown
parameter theta I.

280
00:14:05,310 --> 00:14:06,700
So let's just do a quick check.

281
00:14:06,700 --> 00:14:08,855
Your understanding about
the framework of bandits.

282
00:15:15,650 --> 00:15:16,150
OK.

283
00:15:16,150 --> 00:15:16,540
Great.

284
00:15:16,540 --> 00:15:18,707
I think most people are
converging on these already.

285
00:15:18,707 --> 00:15:19,710
Yes.

286
00:15:19,710 --> 00:15:22,050
Pulling an arm or
taking an action

287
00:15:22,050 --> 00:15:23,955
is just the action
we're actually doing.

288
00:15:23,955 --> 00:15:26,580
The second one, this is a better
fit to the problem than an MDP

289
00:15:26,580 --> 00:15:29,118
because we're only going to
make one decision per patient.

290
00:15:29,118 --> 00:15:31,410
And we're also going to assume
that whatever decision--

291
00:15:31,410 --> 00:15:35,040
whether [INAUDIBLE]
toe heals after we

292
00:15:35,040 --> 00:15:37,260
do this is independent of
whether or not when Sophie

293
00:15:37,260 --> 00:15:38,660
shows up, what we do.

294
00:15:38,660 --> 00:15:42,255
So these are totally
independent processes.

295
00:15:42,255 --> 00:15:43,380
The next person to show up.

296
00:15:43,380 --> 00:15:45,430
So we don't have any
sequential dependence,

297
00:15:45,430 --> 00:15:47,472
even though we're making
a sequence of decisions.

298
00:15:47,472 --> 00:15:49,600
It's like, each time point,
there's a new person.

299
00:15:49,600 --> 00:15:51,810
We're just going to decide
what to do for them.

300
00:15:51,810 --> 00:15:53,110
And yes, this is right.

301
00:15:53,110 --> 00:15:55,390
So if your theta I
is between 0 and 1,

302
00:15:55,390 --> 00:15:57,440
meaning your outcomes
are not deterministic,

303
00:15:57,440 --> 00:16:00,780
sometimes you'll heal,
sometimes you won't.

304
00:16:00,780 --> 00:16:02,018
OK.

305
00:16:02,018 --> 00:16:03,810
So one thing that we
could do to solve this

306
00:16:03,810 --> 00:16:05,250
would be to use-- yeah.

307
00:16:05,250 --> 00:16:09,570
So to confirm there is no
time point dependence you have

308
00:16:09,570 --> 00:16:10,960
to probability distribution.

309
00:16:10,960 --> 00:16:13,620
It has to be the same in
every single [INAUDIBLE].

310
00:16:13,620 --> 00:16:14,290
Great question.

311
00:16:14,290 --> 00:16:16,280
We're going to assume for now
that everything's stationary,

312
00:16:16,280 --> 00:16:18,490
meaning that reward
probability distribution is

313
00:16:18,490 --> 00:16:20,010
the same at every time step.

314
00:16:20,010 --> 00:16:22,150
So there's lots of really
interesting questions

315
00:16:22,150 --> 00:16:23,123
around nonstationarity.

316
00:16:23,123 --> 00:16:24,290
Our labs don't work on that.

317
00:16:24,290 --> 00:16:26,540
There's lots of other really
interesting work on this,

318
00:16:26,540 --> 00:16:28,660
like with time point
detection and change points.

319
00:16:28,660 --> 00:16:30,050
For now, we're going to
see about stationary.

320
00:16:30,050 --> 00:16:32,300
And that would include the
fact that we don't suddenly

321
00:16:32,300 --> 00:16:36,010
get a new distribution of people
for whom different things work.

322
00:16:36,010 --> 00:16:37,890
Good question.

323
00:16:37,890 --> 00:16:38,390
All right.

324
00:16:38,390 --> 00:16:41,070
So one thing you could imagine
doing is just to be greedy.

325
00:16:41,070 --> 00:16:43,570
So what we're going to do in
this case, we're going to use Q

326
00:16:43,570 --> 00:16:46,930
today not to denote a state
action or discounted sum

327
00:16:46,930 --> 00:16:49,503
of future rewards, or you
can think of it like that,

328
00:16:49,503 --> 00:16:51,670
except for there's no state,
there's a single state.

329
00:16:51,670 --> 00:16:55,930
And it's only over actions and
it's only the immediate reward.

330
00:16:55,930 --> 00:16:58,600
So what Q here would
denote is what is just

331
00:16:58,600 --> 00:17:01,674
the expected reward of RMA?

332
00:17:01,674 --> 00:17:04,720
And we can just estimate
that by counting.

333
00:17:04,720 --> 00:17:06,460
We can just look
up every other time

334
00:17:06,460 --> 00:17:09,650
we did surgery, what were the
outcomes for that individual?

335
00:17:09,650 --> 00:17:10,730
And we can average.

336
00:17:10,730 --> 00:17:12,339
And what the greedy
algorithm does

337
00:17:12,339 --> 00:17:15,550
is they just selects the
action with the highest value

338
00:17:15,550 --> 00:17:18,809
and takes that action, observes
the outcome, and repeats.

339
00:17:21,460 --> 00:17:23,930
So let's think about what
happens when we do that.

340
00:17:23,930 --> 00:17:26,650
So if you have this setting--

341
00:17:26,650 --> 00:17:29,750
imagine that this really is
the true set of parameters.

342
00:17:29,750 --> 00:17:32,690
So surgery, in this case,
in our fake example,

343
00:17:32,690 --> 00:17:35,500
is actually the most effective
buddy taping the second,

344
00:17:35,500 --> 00:17:39,040
and doing nothing is
not very effective.

345
00:17:39,040 --> 00:17:41,548
So imagine this.

346
00:17:41,548 --> 00:17:43,840
So you start off-- and this
is pretty common with a lot

347
00:17:43,840 --> 00:17:45,140
of bandit algorithms.

348
00:17:45,140 --> 00:17:47,195
If you have a small,
finite set of actions,

349
00:17:47,195 --> 00:17:49,820
often, you'll just start off and
you'll sample everything once.

350
00:17:49,820 --> 00:17:52,760
Now, when you start to get into
really large action spaces,

351
00:17:52,760 --> 00:17:55,420
like, all of the ads you
could recommend to people,

352
00:17:55,420 --> 00:17:56,930
we'll have to do
something smarter.

353
00:17:56,930 --> 00:17:59,410
But in this case, you can just
sample all the actions once,

354
00:17:59,410 --> 00:18:02,110
and let's see what
you would observe.

355
00:18:02,110 --> 00:18:04,120
So in this case,
imagine that you

356
00:18:04,120 --> 00:18:07,370
get the first observation
here is 0 for arm one,

357
00:18:07,370 --> 00:18:10,420
it's 1 for arm 2,
and 0 for arm 3.

358
00:18:10,420 --> 00:18:13,520
So which arm-- this is
not meant to be tricky.

359
00:18:13,520 --> 00:18:16,010
Which arm would you select next
under the greedy algorithm?

360
00:18:25,430 --> 00:18:27,878
And which of them
has the highest?

361
00:18:27,878 --> 00:18:28,770
[INAUDIBLE]

362
00:18:28,770 --> 00:18:29,270
Great.

363
00:18:29,270 --> 00:18:29,820
Exactly.

364
00:18:29,820 --> 00:18:31,790
So you would just--
there would be--

365
00:18:31,790 --> 00:18:34,580
deterministically, the
probability of picking a 2

366
00:18:34,580 --> 00:18:35,900
would be equal to 1.

367
00:18:35,900 --> 00:18:39,350
You're just determined to
take whichever one looks best.

368
00:18:39,350 --> 00:18:41,770
So would that be good or bad?

369
00:18:46,710 --> 00:18:47,210
Bad.

370
00:18:47,210 --> 00:18:51,350
And in particular, would you
ever select the optimal action?

371
00:18:51,350 --> 00:18:52,010
No.

372
00:18:52,010 --> 00:18:53,720
So you actually couldn't--

373
00:18:53,720 --> 00:18:58,160
so it will never
find it, because you

374
00:18:58,160 --> 00:19:02,250
have a really low estimate of
the true value of these two.

375
00:19:02,250 --> 00:19:05,840
Your average for a2 can
never drop down to 0,

376
00:19:05,840 --> 00:19:07,380
because you've
got at least 1, 1.

377
00:19:07,380 --> 00:19:09,210
And so even if you
get 0s forever,

378
00:19:09,210 --> 00:19:11,100
which you're unlikely
to get two for a2,

379
00:19:11,100 --> 00:19:13,730
you're never going
to sample a 1 again.

380
00:19:13,730 --> 00:19:15,410
So what we would
say in this case

381
00:19:15,410 --> 00:19:17,360
is that this means
that you will not

382
00:19:17,360 --> 00:19:19,760
converge to the optimal
action, and this algorithm

383
00:19:19,760 --> 00:19:20,820
is not very good.

384
00:19:20,820 --> 00:19:24,380
And we'll formalize what we mean
by not very good in a second.

385
00:19:24,380 --> 00:19:26,660
So this just is to illustrate
why you should not just

386
00:19:26,660 --> 00:19:30,050
be greedy, that you can lock
on to the suboptimal action

387
00:19:30,050 --> 00:19:30,780
forever.

388
00:19:30,780 --> 00:19:35,000
This highlights why you need
to do some form of exploration

389
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:37,820
because you can, in fact,
make an infinite number

390
00:19:37,820 --> 00:19:39,530
of bad decisions.

391
00:19:39,530 --> 00:19:41,690
So how do we quantify
what it means

392
00:19:41,690 --> 00:19:44,300
to make an infinite number
of good or bad decisions?

393
00:19:44,300 --> 00:19:46,330
We're going to use
the word regret.

394
00:19:46,330 --> 00:19:48,080
And we're going to
mean regret in the case

395
00:19:48,080 --> 00:19:52,875
of sequential
decision making, OK?

396
00:19:52,875 --> 00:19:55,070
So the idea in this
case is that we're

397
00:19:55,070 --> 00:19:56,895
going to think
formally about, what

398
00:19:56,895 --> 00:19:58,520
is the difference
between the decisions

399
00:19:58,520 --> 00:20:01,430
that our algorithm makes
and the optimal decisions?

400
00:20:01,430 --> 00:20:03,260
And then we're going
to score the algorithm

401
00:20:03,260 --> 00:20:05,450
based on what the gap is.

402
00:20:05,450 --> 00:20:10,087
So in particular,
the optimal value,

403
00:20:10,087 --> 00:20:11,670
just like what we've
seen in the past,

404
00:20:11,670 --> 00:20:13,680
is the maximum
overall, the Q value,

405
00:20:13,680 --> 00:20:17,630
so it's whichever arm has the
best, highest expected reward,

406
00:20:17,630 --> 00:20:20,960
and the regret is
the opportunity loss.

407
00:20:20,960 --> 00:20:22,960
You could also think of
this as the difference--

408
00:20:22,960 --> 00:20:24,007
is the advantage.

409
00:20:24,007 --> 00:20:25,840
The advantage of the
optimal action compared

410
00:20:25,840 --> 00:20:28,000
to the action that's taken.

411
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,620
And so your regret, just like
we often use it colloquially,

412
00:20:30,620 --> 00:20:34,480
is the gap between what the
agent could have achieved

413
00:20:34,480 --> 00:20:36,760
and what it actually got.

414
00:20:36,760 --> 00:20:40,010
We're going to focus here of
looking at these in expectation.

415
00:20:40,010 --> 00:20:41,890
Of course, due to
stochasticity, there

416
00:20:41,890 --> 00:20:43,960
could be times where
the particular reward

417
00:20:43,960 --> 00:20:46,180
you get for a suboptimal
action might be higher

418
00:20:46,180 --> 00:20:47,150
than the action--

419
00:20:47,150 --> 00:20:49,030
the reward you'd get
for the optimal action

420
00:20:49,030 --> 00:20:50,560
because of stochasticity.

421
00:20:50,560 --> 00:20:52,825
But we're just going to
focus here on expectations.

422
00:20:52,825 --> 00:20:54,700
So we're always comparing
the expected reward

423
00:20:54,700 --> 00:20:56,290
of the optimal arm
to the expected

424
00:20:56,290 --> 00:20:59,410
reward of the suboptimal arm.

425
00:20:59,410 --> 00:21:00,910
So that's regret.

426
00:21:00,910 --> 00:21:03,400
So how do we compute it?

427
00:21:03,400 --> 00:21:07,210
We're going to think about
comparing this over all time

428
00:21:07,210 --> 00:21:08,890
steps, and we're
going to maximize

429
00:21:08,890 --> 00:21:11,380
cumulative reward, which
is equivalent to minimizing

430
00:21:11,380 --> 00:21:12,850
total regret.

431
00:21:12,850 --> 00:21:17,470
Because remember, this is
unknown, but it's fixed.

432
00:21:17,470 --> 00:21:21,000
So we really want to
maximize our total reward,

433
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:25,100
and we can either think of
that as you're maximizing the Q

434
00:21:25,100 --> 00:21:26,840
you got over all
time steps or we're

435
00:21:26,840 --> 00:21:29,100
minimizing the total regret.

436
00:21:29,100 --> 00:21:31,850
And normally in bandits, we talk
about minimizing total regret

437
00:21:31,850 --> 00:21:34,570
instead of maximizing
total reward.

438
00:21:34,570 --> 00:21:35,070
All right.

439
00:21:35,070 --> 00:21:38,540
Let's see how we can think about
how big the regret will be.

440
00:21:38,540 --> 00:21:43,100
So let's let Nt(a) be
the number of times

441
00:21:43,100 --> 00:21:50,720
action a has been
selected at time step t.

442
00:21:50,720 --> 00:21:53,760
So that means that if your
agent has made t decisions,

443
00:21:53,760 --> 00:21:57,030
you count up and see, how many
times did I take action a1?

444
00:21:57,030 --> 00:21:58,530
How many times did
I take action a2?

445
00:21:58,530 --> 00:22:00,980
How many times did
I take action a3?

446
00:22:00,980 --> 00:22:04,160
The gap for a particular
arm is essentially

447
00:22:04,160 --> 00:22:11,740
its advantage of a star over a.

448
00:22:11,740 --> 00:22:13,870
So it's just the
difference between what

449
00:22:13,870 --> 00:22:16,510
is the expected reward the
optimal action would have gotten

450
00:22:16,510 --> 00:22:18,370
minus the expected
reward you get

451
00:22:18,370 --> 00:22:20,330
under this alternative action?

452
00:22:20,330 --> 00:22:22,010
And we often call this the gap.

453
00:22:22,010 --> 00:22:25,100
I think the literature developed
somewhat independently,

454
00:22:25,100 --> 00:22:27,280
and so I think that's
why people don't commonly

455
00:22:27,280 --> 00:22:28,280
call it the advantage.

456
00:22:28,280 --> 00:22:30,968
In the case of bandits, they
typically call it the gap.

457
00:22:30,968 --> 00:22:33,010
And the gap will turn out
to be pretty important,

458
00:22:33,010 --> 00:22:36,920
because as you might start
to think about intuitively,

459
00:22:36,920 --> 00:22:38,630
depending on the
size of the gap,

460
00:22:38,630 --> 00:22:40,510
it's going to be easier
or harder to learn

461
00:22:40,510 --> 00:22:42,520
which of two actions is better.

462
00:22:42,520 --> 00:22:45,760
So if the gaps are really large
between action 1 and action

463
00:22:45,760 --> 00:22:48,422
2, which means they have really
different expected rewards,

464
00:22:48,422 --> 00:22:50,630
you're going to need less
samples to figure that out.

465
00:22:50,630 --> 00:22:52,630
If the gaps are really,
really small, generally,

466
00:22:52,630 --> 00:22:54,570
you need a lot more data, OK?

467
00:22:54,570 --> 00:22:56,320
So again, it's going
to just be a function

468
00:22:56,320 --> 00:22:57,590
of the gaps and the accounts.

469
00:22:57,590 --> 00:23:00,700
So we can just think
of the number of times

470
00:23:00,700 --> 00:23:03,730
that you took each action
and the difference between--

471
00:23:03,730 --> 00:23:04,422
and this gap.

472
00:23:04,422 --> 00:23:06,130
The difference between
the optimal action

473
00:23:06,130 --> 00:23:09,300
you should have taken and
the reward you actually got.

474
00:23:09,300 --> 00:23:12,320
And so our expected
regret here is just

475
00:23:12,320 --> 00:23:19,410
going to be the sum of times you
take each action times the gap.

476
00:23:19,410 --> 00:23:21,240
And so what that
means intuitively

477
00:23:21,240 --> 00:23:24,900
is that we do not want
to take actions which

478
00:23:24,900 --> 00:23:27,330
have large gaps
very much, and it's

479
00:23:27,330 --> 00:23:29,310
more OK if we take more
of actions that are

480
00:23:29,310 --> 00:23:31,740
close to the optimal action.

481
00:23:31,740 --> 00:23:34,480
And a lot of algorithms--

482
00:23:34,480 --> 00:23:36,480
for a lot of algorithms,
what we try to do is we

483
00:23:36,480 --> 00:23:37,970
try to bound this quantity.

484
00:23:37,970 --> 00:23:39,760
So we try to say, in
advance-- in general,

485
00:23:39,760 --> 00:23:41,260
this is something
that we can't know

486
00:23:41,260 --> 00:23:43,380
because this requires
access to what is ever

487
00:23:43,380 --> 00:23:45,732
is the optimal
action and its value.

488
00:23:45,732 --> 00:23:47,440
And we don't know
either of those things.

489
00:23:47,440 --> 00:23:48,750
But what we can
do is we can have

490
00:23:48,750 --> 00:23:50,375
algorithms where we
can prove something

491
00:23:50,375 --> 00:23:52,300
about how the regret grows.

492
00:23:52,300 --> 00:23:53,910
OK.

493
00:23:53,910 --> 00:23:54,410
All right.

494
00:23:54,410 --> 00:23:57,250
Let's just see what I mean
just to instantiate that.

495
00:23:57,250 --> 00:23:58,175
OK.

496
00:23:58,175 --> 00:24:00,050
So again, we can't do
this in the real world,

497
00:24:00,050 --> 00:24:04,427
but we can do this
for a toy example.

498
00:24:04,427 --> 00:24:06,010
Let's just think
about what the regret

499
00:24:06,010 --> 00:24:07,510
would look like in this case.

500
00:24:07,510 --> 00:24:10,000
So this would be
a series-- if you

501
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:11,750
were running your
greedy algorithm,

502
00:24:11,750 --> 00:24:13,450
so this is the actions.

503
00:24:13,450 --> 00:24:14,570
This is time.

504
00:24:14,570 --> 00:24:17,620
This is 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

505
00:24:17,620 --> 00:24:20,720
So we first take
all of our actions.

506
00:24:20,720 --> 00:24:23,750
In each of those cases, the
true optimal action was a 1,

507
00:24:23,750 --> 00:24:27,710
and our observed reward was 1
and our regret was as follows.

508
00:24:27,710 --> 00:24:30,680
So a1 really is
the optimal action,

509
00:24:30,680 --> 00:24:32,740
so we have 0 regret there.

510
00:24:32,740 --> 00:24:37,760
The second one, our
regret was this.

511
00:24:37,760 --> 00:24:40,015
And for the third one,
our regret was this.

512
00:24:46,410 --> 00:24:49,600
So this just shows you
what the size would be.

513
00:24:49,600 --> 00:24:53,830
And so this here is
actually the gap.

514
00:24:53,830 --> 00:24:56,220
It's the gap between the
optimal arm and the arm

515
00:24:56,220 --> 00:24:57,660
that you're taking.

516
00:24:57,660 --> 00:25:00,040
So this just shows you
how regret can grow.

517
00:25:00,040 --> 00:25:03,640
And as you might expect, if
you make bad decisions forever,

518
00:25:03,640 --> 00:25:05,940
you're going to
get linear regret.

519
00:25:05,940 --> 00:25:08,470
So for example here
in the greedy case,

520
00:25:08,470 --> 00:25:11,520
if we now take a3
forever, our regret

521
00:25:11,520 --> 00:25:15,780
is going to be the total number
of time steps t times 0.85,

522
00:25:15,780 --> 00:25:17,280
because that's how
much we're losing

523
00:25:17,280 --> 00:25:25,300
for every single decision, and
then we sum them all up, OK?

524
00:25:25,300 --> 00:25:26,990
All right.

525
00:25:26,990 --> 00:25:29,520
So in general, it can linear
in the number of decisions.

526
00:25:29,520 --> 00:25:32,240
And so part of-- the main thing
we're going to be trying to do

527
00:25:32,240 --> 00:25:35,760
is ideally, you would have
constant regret or zero regret.

528
00:25:35,760 --> 00:25:37,280
What I mean by
constant regret would

529
00:25:37,280 --> 00:25:40,760
mean that you make a finite
number of bad decisions.

530
00:25:40,760 --> 00:25:43,202
So if you can figure out what
the optimal arm is and then

531
00:25:43,202 --> 00:25:44,660
take that forever,
then you'll have

532
00:25:44,660 --> 00:25:46,820
constant regret, because
it just is going to be,

533
00:25:46,820 --> 00:25:49,640
say, I make 10 decisions,
then I learn the optimal arm,

534
00:25:49,640 --> 00:25:51,540
and then I make the
optimal thing forever.

535
00:25:51,540 --> 00:25:54,950
That's generally
pretty hard to do.

536
00:25:54,950 --> 00:25:56,640
In the worst case,
you'll be linear.

537
00:25:56,640 --> 00:26:00,270
You'll make a mistake on every
single arm decision forever.

538
00:26:00,270 --> 00:26:02,030
And typically, what
we're hoping to find

539
00:26:02,030 --> 00:26:04,230
is we're hoping to
have sublinear regret.

540
00:26:04,230 --> 00:26:05,960
So it still might
grow with the number

541
00:26:05,960 --> 00:26:08,430
of time steps, the number
of decisions you're making,

542
00:26:08,430 --> 00:26:11,220
but it's not going to be linear.

543
00:26:11,220 --> 00:26:11,720
OK.

544
00:26:11,720 --> 00:26:14,240
And we'll see a lot
more about that.

545
00:26:14,240 --> 00:26:16,700
OK.

