659
00:32:48,070 --> 00:32:49,710
And so that can
matter a lot, too.

660
00:32:49,710 --> 00:32:52,910
But static epsilon is not great.

661
00:32:52,910 --> 00:32:53,410
All right.

662
00:32:53,410 --> 00:32:56,970
So let's look at what
this can look like.

663
00:32:56,970 --> 00:32:59,710
If you think about how regret
is growing over time steps,

664
00:32:59,710 --> 00:33:01,090
these are very common plots.

665
00:33:01,090 --> 00:33:03,763
When you look at bandits
or some other approaches,

666
00:33:03,763 --> 00:33:05,680
we'll see if we consider
what total regret is,

667
00:33:05,680 --> 00:33:08,130
you'd like a regret to be 0.

668
00:33:08,130 --> 00:33:11,202
If you make it greedy,
it can be linear.

669
00:33:11,202 --> 00:33:12,660
If you make it
epsilon greedy, it's

670
00:33:12,660 --> 00:33:15,090
normally a little bit better,
but it's still linear.

671
00:33:15,090 --> 00:33:19,170
If you decay it, it
can get a lot closer.

672
00:33:19,170 --> 00:33:22,470
And it is going to be
possible to be sublinear

673
00:33:22,470 --> 00:33:25,620
for good choices of algorithms.

674
00:33:25,620 --> 00:33:27,390
One of the challenges
for this is

675
00:33:27,390 --> 00:33:30,330
that it can turn out that there
can be some pretty good choices

676
00:33:30,330 --> 00:33:33,570
of epsilon, but it often
depends on dependent properties

677
00:33:33,570 --> 00:33:35,230
that we don't in advance.

678
00:33:35,230 --> 00:33:37,440
So we need to have
an algorithm, which

679
00:33:37,440 --> 00:33:40,830
before knowing how anything
about the problem in terms

680
00:33:40,830 --> 00:33:42,600
of the gaps or
anything like that,

681
00:33:42,600 --> 00:33:45,930
can be guaranteed to
have sublinear regret.

682
00:33:45,930 --> 00:33:50,070
So first of all, let's think
about what type of regret bounds

683
00:33:50,070 --> 00:33:52,990
we might get and is
there reasons for hope?

684
00:33:52,990 --> 00:33:55,807
So a problem-independent
bound talks about,

685
00:33:55,807 --> 00:33:57,390
how does the regret
grow as a function

686
00:33:57,390 --> 00:34:02,940
of t for any possible problem
that you might be given?

687
00:34:02,940 --> 00:34:05,070
So what this might say
is, I might give you

688
00:34:05,070 --> 00:34:07,320
an algorithm which is
guaranteed to be sublinear

689
00:34:07,320 --> 00:34:10,162
in t no matter what bandit
problem you put me in.

690
00:34:10,162 --> 00:34:12,120
So that's just an algorithm
that will work well

691
00:34:12,120 --> 00:34:14,790
for any potential
domain you put in,

692
00:34:14,790 --> 00:34:17,580
and it'll make a bound
on its performance.

693
00:34:17,580 --> 00:34:21,179
Instance-dependent or
problem-dependent bounds

694
00:34:21,179 --> 00:34:25,230
bound things as a
function of the gap.

695
00:34:25,230 --> 00:34:26,730
And one of the
really elegant things

696
00:34:26,730 --> 00:34:28,469
of problem-dependent
bounds is that it

697
00:34:28,469 --> 00:34:30,639
doesn't mean the algorithm
has to know the gaps.

698
00:34:30,639 --> 00:34:33,670
It just means that if it
turns out the problem is easy,

699
00:34:33,670 --> 00:34:35,639
like, there are
really large gaps,

700
00:34:35,639 --> 00:34:37,739
you will have a
much better regret.

701
00:34:37,739 --> 00:34:42,179
And so some of my labs work and
a number of other people too,

702
00:34:42,179 --> 00:34:43,929
were often very
interested in this.

703
00:34:43,929 --> 00:34:44,969
And I think at a
high level, what

704
00:34:44,969 --> 00:34:46,719
this means is you have
an algorithm that's

705
00:34:46,719 --> 00:34:47,800
adaptive to the problem.

706
00:34:47,800 --> 00:34:49,139
So it means that
your algorithm will

707
00:34:49,139 --> 00:34:51,130
be guaranteed to do
really well on the problem

708
00:34:51,130 --> 00:34:52,810
if the problem is
easier to learn in.

709
00:34:52,810 --> 00:34:55,620
And if it's harder, well,
then you can't do well anyway.

710
00:34:55,620 --> 00:34:57,430
It'll do as well as it can.

711
00:34:57,430 --> 00:35:00,980
So we'll talk about bounds
that are both types of these.

712
00:35:00,980 --> 00:35:05,960
In general, is the gap
usually less than the value

713
00:35:05,960 --> 00:35:09,880
if we're considering
only the words?

714
00:35:09,880 --> 00:35:13,245
And we usually consider only
rewards between 0 and 1?

715
00:35:13,245 --> 00:35:13,870
Great question.

716
00:35:13,870 --> 00:35:15,400
Totally depends on the domain.

717
00:35:15,400 --> 00:35:17,890
So if you're looking at
Bernoulli's, then it's

718
00:35:17,890 --> 00:35:20,117
naturally between 0 and 1.

719
00:35:20,117 --> 00:35:21,700
Other domains might
be very different.

720
00:35:21,700 --> 00:35:22,980
You can always normalize it.

721
00:35:22,980 --> 00:35:26,420
I think whether the domain has
really big gaps really depends.

722
00:35:26,420 --> 00:35:29,607
So if you think about something
click through rates for ads,

723
00:35:29,607 --> 00:35:31,940
click through rates are really,
really hard to optimize.

724
00:35:31,940 --> 00:35:36,100
It's often like
0.01 versus 0.011.

725
00:35:36,100 --> 00:35:37,160
Nobody likes ads.

726
00:35:37,160 --> 00:35:39,980
So in those cases,
the differences,

727
00:35:39,980 --> 00:35:42,508
the gaps we're looking at,
could often be really tiny.

728
00:35:42,508 --> 00:35:44,300
And so you'll generally
need a lot of data,

729
00:35:44,300 --> 00:35:48,100
and having smart data efficient
algorithms will matter a lot.

730
00:35:48,100 --> 00:35:51,410
There might be other cases
where there's really big gaps.

731
00:35:51,410 --> 00:35:54,680
If the problem has really
big gaps, it's really easy.

732
00:35:54,680 --> 00:35:57,760
And so it tends to not matter
too much what you do there,

733
00:35:57,760 --> 00:35:59,380
because you can
quickly estimate them.

734
00:35:59,380 --> 00:36:00,910
Great question.

735
00:36:00,910 --> 00:36:03,070
OK.

736
00:36:03,070 --> 00:36:03,590
All right.

737
00:36:03,590 --> 00:36:05,290
So here's a reason for hope.

738
00:36:05,290 --> 00:36:08,000
So there's a nice lower
bound by Lai and Robins--

739
00:36:08,000 --> 00:36:11,310
I think this was around
1950s, it's been a long time,

740
00:36:11,310 --> 00:36:14,570
which tries to think about,
what's the minimum regret

741
00:36:14,570 --> 00:36:18,230
you're going to get as a
function of the problem?

742
00:36:18,230 --> 00:36:20,270
And so this means
that any algorithm

743
00:36:20,270 --> 00:36:23,480
is going to suffer at least
this much in terms of regret.

744
00:36:23,480 --> 00:36:27,483
So it says, you're going
to at least be log t,

745
00:36:27,483 --> 00:36:30,150
like, the number of time steps,
number of decisions you've made.

746
00:36:30,150 --> 00:36:33,420
And for any arm for
which it is suboptimal,

747
00:36:33,420 --> 00:36:36,500
you're going to suffer this
in terms of a KL difference

748
00:36:36,500 --> 00:36:39,920
between your distribution of
rewards you get on your arm

749
00:36:39,920 --> 00:36:42,070
on that arm versus
the optimal arm

750
00:36:42,070 --> 00:36:45,170
and with the gap
on the numerator.

