00:36:45,170 --> 00:36:48,600
But this should be promising
because it's sublinear.

752
00:36:48,600 --> 00:36:49,560
It's log.

753
00:36:49,560 --> 00:36:50,640
It's not linear.

754
00:36:50,640 --> 00:36:54,480
Which means that the lower
bound says, according to this,

755
00:36:54,480 --> 00:36:58,160
it is not yet impossible to try
to have sublinear regret, OK?

756
00:36:58,160 --> 00:37:00,500
And this would be considered
a problem-dependent

757
00:37:00,500 --> 00:37:02,690
or incident-dependent
bound because this holds

758
00:37:02,690 --> 00:37:03,940
based on the unknown gaps.

759
00:37:06,560 --> 00:37:08,377
OK.

760
00:37:08,377 --> 00:37:10,460
So now we're going to see
one of my favorite ideas

761
00:37:10,460 --> 00:37:13,320
in the course, which is
optimism under uncertainty,

762
00:37:13,320 --> 00:37:15,940
which gives us--

763
00:37:15,940 --> 00:37:17,690
I think it's a lovely
principle because it

764
00:37:17,690 --> 00:37:20,940
shows why it's provably optimal
to be optimistic about things,

765
00:37:20,940 --> 00:37:22,657
which is beautiful.

766
00:37:22,657 --> 00:37:24,740
And it's going to be one
of the first things we're

767
00:37:24,740 --> 00:37:26,157
going to see that's
going to allow

768
00:37:26,157 --> 00:37:27,324
us to have sublinear regret.

769
00:37:27,324 --> 00:37:27,824
OK.

770
00:37:27,824 --> 00:37:29,540
So why is optimism
good and what do we

771
00:37:29,540 --> 00:37:31,460
mean by optimism in this case?

772
00:37:31,460 --> 00:37:36,440
What we mean is, we're going
to choose actions or arms,

773
00:37:36,440 --> 00:37:41,060
some typo there, that
might have a high value.

774
00:37:41,060 --> 00:37:45,560
Well, what happens when we
choose things that are good?

775
00:37:45,560 --> 00:37:52,055
So one thing that can happen is
we actually get high reward, OK?

776
00:37:52,055 --> 00:37:54,180
So that's good, because
that's our goal, because we

777
00:37:54,180 --> 00:37:55,200
want to get high reward.

778
00:37:55,200 --> 00:37:57,310
We want to maximize
reward/minimize cost.

779
00:37:57,310 --> 00:37:59,352
What's the other thing
that can happen if we pick

780
00:37:59,352 --> 00:38:01,237
something that might be good?

781
00:38:01,237 --> 00:38:02,195
Might have high reward.

782
00:38:10,550 --> 00:38:12,360
Low reward.

783
00:38:12,360 --> 00:38:12,930
Exactly.

784
00:38:12,930 --> 00:38:13,430
OK.

785
00:38:13,430 --> 00:38:14,760
Yeah, those are the
only two things,

786
00:38:14,760 --> 00:38:16,802
you can either get higher
or you can lower award.

787
00:38:16,802 --> 00:38:18,977
What happens if
there's low reward?

788
00:38:18,977 --> 00:38:20,310
I mean, of course, there's that.

789
00:38:20,310 --> 00:38:22,140
But aside from
that, what happens,

790
00:38:22,140 --> 00:38:24,230
do you think, probably
to our estimates,

791
00:38:24,230 --> 00:38:26,310
those Q estimates if
we get low reward?

792
00:38:26,310 --> 00:38:26,840
Yeah.

793
00:38:26,840 --> 00:38:29,330
[INAUDIBLE]

794
00:38:29,330 --> 00:38:29,960
Exactly.

795
00:38:29,960 --> 00:38:30,900
Yeah, exactly.

796
00:38:30,900 --> 00:38:32,500
Remind me your name.

797
00:38:32,500 --> 00:38:33,000
Yeah.

798
00:38:33,000 --> 00:38:34,450
So what you said
is exactly right.

799
00:38:34,450 --> 00:38:38,400
So basically, either
you get high reward

800
00:38:38,400 --> 00:38:39,810
or you learned
something new, OK?

801
00:38:39,810 --> 00:38:42,432
So the other alternative
is you get low reward

802
00:38:42,432 --> 00:38:43,890
and you learn
something, and you're

803
00:38:43,890 --> 00:38:47,010
going to improve your estimates.

804
00:38:47,010 --> 00:38:49,080
And from the point of
view of a reinforcement

805
00:38:49,080 --> 00:38:51,570
learning algorithm or abandoned
algorithm, both of these

806
00:38:51,570 --> 00:38:52,517
are really valuable.

807
00:38:52,517 --> 00:38:54,600
Because either you're
actually achieving your goal

808
00:38:54,600 --> 00:38:56,970
or you are learning something
so that in the future,

809
00:38:56,970 --> 00:38:59,230
you won't make bad
decisions in the future, OK?

810
00:38:59,230 --> 00:39:01,560
So that is why optimism
is-- we're going

811
00:39:01,560 --> 00:39:05,450
to see provably optimal, OK?

812
00:39:05,450 --> 00:39:05,950
All right.

813
00:39:05,950 --> 00:39:06,690
Now, of course,
that means that we

814
00:39:06,690 --> 00:39:09,270
have to have an algorithm that
leverages the information we

815
00:39:09,270 --> 00:39:11,193
get when we see low rewards.

816
00:39:11,193 --> 00:39:13,110
So we're going to have
to be formal about what

817
00:39:13,110 --> 00:39:14,670
it means to might.

818
00:39:14,670 --> 00:39:20,877
We're going to formalize this
as quantifying our uncertainty.

819
00:39:20,877 --> 00:39:23,210
So we're going to need to be
precise over our confidence

820
00:39:23,210 --> 00:39:24,918
intervals or uncertainty
bounds, and then

821
00:39:24,918 --> 00:39:27,050
use that to make decisions.

822
00:39:27,050 --> 00:39:28,273
OK.

823
00:39:28,273 --> 00:39:29,690
So in particular,
what we're going

824
00:39:29,690 --> 00:39:31,430
to do is we are
going to estimate

825
00:39:31,430 --> 00:39:33,320
an upper confidence
bound for each action

826
00:39:33,320 --> 00:39:36,510
value such that that
confidence bound--

827
00:39:36,510 --> 00:39:39,680
that upper confidence bounds
holds with high probability.

828
00:39:39,680 --> 00:39:41,240
So we're going to
make sure-- we're

829
00:39:41,240 --> 00:39:42,270
going to be frequentist today.

830
00:39:42,270 --> 00:39:43,530
We're not going to be Bayesians.

831
00:39:43,530 --> 00:39:45,570
Don't worry if you haven't
done a lot on either of those.

832
00:39:45,570 --> 00:39:48,480
But we're going to focus today
on just high probability bounds.

833
00:39:48,480 --> 00:39:50,510
So we're going to
need a Ut of a,

834
00:39:50,510 --> 00:39:53,850
where that holds with
high probability,

835
00:39:53,850 --> 00:39:55,550
and we're going
to want this to be

836
00:39:55,550 --> 00:39:58,550
dependent on how many times
we've selected the arm.

837
00:39:58,550 --> 00:40:00,630
There are lots of ways
to quantify uncertainty.

838
00:40:00,630 --> 00:40:02,630
We're going to focus today
on a frequentist view

839
00:40:02,630 --> 00:40:04,580
and just thinking about counts.

840
00:40:04,580 --> 00:40:06,330
And then the way
we're going to behave,

841
00:40:06,330 --> 00:40:08,330
the way that our agent
is going to take actions,

842
00:40:08,330 --> 00:40:10,580
is just going to pick whichever
action has the highest

843
00:40:10,580 --> 00:40:12,440
upper confidence bound.

844
00:40:12,440 --> 00:40:14,510
And there's a whole
suite of algorithms

845
00:40:14,510 --> 00:40:16,246
that are called UCB algorithms.

846
00:40:16,246 --> 00:40:17,780
So there are many
algorithms that

847
00:40:17,780 --> 00:40:19,240
are variants of this notion.

848
00:40:22,500 --> 00:40:23,940
There's also ones
that are called

849
00:40:23,940 --> 00:40:28,680
Optimism in the Face of
Uncertainty, OFU, OK?

850
00:40:28,680 --> 00:40:30,340
So it's a really simple idea.

851
00:40:30,340 --> 00:40:33,150
And now, the question is going
to be how well does this perform

852
00:40:33,150 --> 00:40:37,320
and how do we quantify
the uncertainty?

853
00:40:37,320 --> 00:40:39,240
So let's go through
Hoeffding's inequality.

854
00:40:39,240 --> 00:40:40,740
We're going to use
it in homework 3,

855
00:40:40,740 --> 00:40:44,590
but I'm curious who has
seen it in previous classes.

856
00:40:44,590 --> 00:40:45,090
OK.

857
00:40:45,090 --> 00:40:46,060
Maybe a couple of people.

858
00:40:46,060 --> 00:40:48,018
But most people, I wouldn't
expect you to know.

859
00:40:48,018 --> 00:40:51,930
So Hoeffding inequality is
a really useful inequality.

860
00:40:51,930 --> 00:40:54,270
The idea of it is we're
just going to think about,

861
00:40:54,270 --> 00:41:00,330
how different can our observed
average be from the true mean?

862
00:41:00,330 --> 00:41:04,410
So let's say we
have n samples that

863
00:41:04,410 --> 00:41:06,480
are somewhere between 0 and 1.

864
00:41:06,480 --> 00:41:09,480
And this is our
true expectation,

865
00:41:09,480 --> 00:41:11,750
so is their true mean, which
we don't know what it is,

866
00:41:11,750 --> 00:41:15,240
and this is our sample mean,
just over the n samples.

867
00:41:15,240 --> 00:41:17,220
What Hoeffding's
inequality says is

868
00:41:17,220 --> 00:41:20,490
that the difference between
your empirical estimate

869
00:41:20,490 --> 00:41:24,870
and the true
estimate, if they're

870
00:41:24,870 --> 00:41:28,080
off by U, then the
probability of that happening

871
00:41:28,080 --> 00:41:29,760
is going down exponentially.

872
00:41:29,760 --> 00:41:34,678
Which essentially means
that as you have more data,

873
00:41:34,678 --> 00:41:36,720
the chance that your
empirical estimate is really

874
00:41:36,720 --> 00:41:41,180
different than your true mean is
going down exponentially fast.

875
00:41:41,180 --> 00:41:44,620
If you can't have your
empirical average be 30

876
00:41:44,620 --> 00:41:47,570
and the real thing is 2000,
if you have a lot of data,

877
00:41:47,570 --> 00:41:51,652
you're going to converge
on the true mean.

878
00:41:51,652 --> 00:41:53,360
Which is, of course,
what you would hope,

879
00:41:53,360 --> 00:41:56,290
but that this is a formal
thing about what the rate is.

880
00:41:56,290 --> 00:41:58,540
So let's just look for a
second and think a little bit

881
00:41:58,540 --> 00:42:00,760
about what this can imply.

882
00:42:00,760 --> 00:42:02,020
So let's look at this part.

883
00:42:02,020 --> 00:42:04,270
Let's say-- I'm going to do
it for the absolute, value

884
00:42:04,270 --> 00:42:09,260
probability of E of X minus
Xn, so this is, again,

885
00:42:09,260 --> 00:42:11,950
just our empirical mean.

886
00:42:11,950 --> 00:42:13,850
The probability this
is greater than mu.

887
00:42:13,850 --> 00:42:16,330
So this gap between
our empirical average

888
00:42:16,330 --> 00:42:17,290
and the true one is.

889
00:42:17,290 --> 00:42:19,790
And so just to back up, why
are we doing all of this?

890
00:42:19,790 --> 00:42:21,165
We're going to
want to figure out

891
00:42:21,165 --> 00:42:25,060
a way to get an upper bound on
what the real mean is of this.

892
00:42:25,060 --> 00:42:26,590
And so what this
equation is going

893
00:42:26,590 --> 00:42:28,420
to allow us to do is
to try to figure out,

894
00:42:28,420 --> 00:42:31,900
how big do we need to set
U to be in order for us

895
00:42:31,900 --> 00:42:36,280
to get an upper bound on what
the true expected reward might

896
00:42:36,280 --> 00:42:37,660
be for a particular arm?

897
00:42:37,660 --> 00:42:38,410
OK.

898
00:42:38,410 --> 00:42:38,910
All right.

899
00:42:38,910 --> 00:42:40,590
So let's see how we can do that.

900
00:42:40,590 --> 00:42:41,090
All right.

901
00:42:41,090 --> 00:42:42,840
So we're going to say,
this is less than--

902
00:42:42,840 --> 00:42:44,560
I've got an absolute
value here, so we're

903
00:42:44,560 --> 00:42:46,373
going to use this version.

904
00:42:48,800 --> 00:42:49,300
OK.

905
00:42:49,300 --> 00:42:50,955
And we're going to
set this to delta.

906
00:42:50,955 --> 00:42:53,080
So this is going to be the
confidence with which we

907
00:42:53,080 --> 00:42:56,500
want this confidence
interval to hold.

908
00:42:56,500 --> 00:43:00,520
So this is going
to be, want the CI

909
00:43:00,520 --> 00:43:05,643
to hold with this probability
with 1 minus delta probability.

910
00:43:05,643 --> 00:43:07,060
So we're going to
try to construct

911
00:43:07,060 --> 00:43:09,580
an upper confidence bound that
holds with least probability

912
00:43:09,580 --> 00:43:11,333
1 minus delta, OK?

913
00:43:11,333 --> 00:43:12,250
So let's just do this.

914
00:43:12,250 --> 00:43:14,458
And now we're going to focus
on this hand, this side.

915
00:43:14,458 --> 00:43:19,780
So we're just going
to do some algebra.

916
00:43:19,780 --> 00:43:21,670
Let's go to delta over 2.

917
00:43:21,670 --> 00:43:26,290
That means U squared is
equal to 1 over n log of 2

918
00:43:26,290 --> 00:43:30,825
over delta, which means U
is equal to square root--

919
00:43:35,300 --> 00:43:36,491
OK.

920
00:43:36,491 --> 00:43:38,150
So this gives us
our range, and it

921
00:43:38,150 --> 00:43:43,010
says if we want the probability
that our empirical estimate

922
00:43:43,010 --> 00:43:48,890
differs from the true
mean by no more than U,

923
00:43:48,890 --> 00:43:53,605
then it is sufficient to
set U equal to this, OK?

924
00:43:53,605 --> 00:43:59,990
So that means that we can
say that Xn minus mu is

925
00:43:59,990 --> 00:44:03,290
less than or equal to
expected value of x, which

926
00:44:03,290 --> 00:44:09,080
is less than or equal to Xn plus
U with probability greater than

927
00:44:09,080 --> 00:44:10,460
or equal to 1 minus delta.

928
00:44:10,460 --> 00:44:13,880
So that just created our
upper confidence bound.

929
00:44:13,880 --> 00:44:16,110
So they said with
high probability,

930
00:44:16,110 --> 00:44:19,610
I can take my empirical
estimate, I added my mu--

931
00:44:19,610 --> 00:44:21,950
my mu here note, just depends
on the number of samples

932
00:44:21,950 --> 00:44:25,225
that I have, and that gives
me my upper confidence bound.

933
00:44:27,770 --> 00:44:29,158
So we can use this.

934
00:44:29,158 --> 00:44:30,450
We can use this given our data.

935
00:44:30,450 --> 00:44:33,158
It just requires us to count how
many times we've sampled things,

936
00:44:33,158 --> 00:44:35,660
compute the average, and then
add on this additional bonus.

937
00:44:35,660 --> 00:44:39,150
We often call these bonus
terms in these cases.

938
00:44:39,150 --> 00:44:40,010
Sorry.

939
00:44:40,010 --> 00:44:43,190
So this is going to
create the UCB1 algorithm.

940
00:44:43,190 --> 00:44:44,840
Which is at every
time step, we're

941
00:44:44,840 --> 00:44:46,882
just going to compute--
this is again-- remember,

942
00:44:46,882 --> 00:44:48,840
the Q hat is this the
empirical average.

943
00:44:56,480 --> 00:45:01,050
And then we add on
this bonus term.

944
00:45:01,050 --> 00:45:02,826
OK.

945
00:45:02,826 --> 00:45:05,640
And this is, again,
just the number

946
00:45:05,640 --> 00:45:12,890
of samples of a
after t time steps.

947
00:45:15,665 --> 00:45:18,060
OK.

948
00:45:18,060 --> 00:45:19,770
And for those of you
familiar with things

949
00:45:19,770 --> 00:45:22,300
like union bonds and stuff,
we'll come to that shortly.

950
00:45:22,300 --> 00:45:24,788
So this is-- we haven't
really fully made

951
00:45:24,788 --> 00:45:26,580
sure that all of these
confidence intervals

952
00:45:26,580 --> 00:45:29,100
are going to hold
over all time steps,

953
00:45:29,100 --> 00:45:31,200
so we'll be a little bit
more careful about what

954
00:45:31,200 --> 00:45:32,470
delta needs to be soon.

955
00:45:32,470 --> 00:45:33,390
Yeah.

956
00:45:33,390 --> 00:45:35,290
It's called UCB1.

957
00:45:35,290 --> 00:45:36,570
Like, why is it 1?

958
00:45:36,570 --> 00:45:38,220
[INAUDIBLE]

959
00:45:38,220 --> 00:45:41,117
There's a lot of different
variants of the UCB algorithm.

960
00:45:41,117 --> 00:45:42,700
I think this is one
of the first ones.

961
00:45:42,700 --> 00:45:48,270
It was, I think,
Auer, U-E-R 2002.

962
00:45:48,270 --> 00:45:52,135
I think it's the one they
named first in their paper.

963
00:45:52,135 --> 00:45:53,560
OK?

964
00:45:53,560 --> 00:45:55,840
But this notion of
optimism under uncertainty

965
00:45:55,840 --> 00:45:58,333
is certainly around
before the 2000.

966
00:45:58,333 --> 00:46:00,250
But I think this is the
paper where they first

967
00:46:00,250 --> 00:46:02,570
did some of these nice proofs.

968
00:46:02,570 --> 00:46:04,730
OK.

969
00:46:04,730 --> 00:46:06,480
All right.

970
00:46:06,480 --> 00:46:06,980
OK.

971
00:46:06,980 --> 00:46:08,720
So let's think
about how different

972
00:46:08,720 --> 00:46:12,446
that algorithm would look like
in our types of settings, OK?

973
00:46:12,446 --> 00:46:16,340
So we're going to use
optimism under uncertainty.

974
00:46:16,340 --> 00:46:20,660
And what we're going
to do in this case is,

975
00:46:20,660 --> 00:46:26,060
we're first going to sample each
arm once, so same as before,

976
00:46:26,060 --> 00:46:28,045
and this is what
we're going to get.

977
00:46:28,045 --> 00:46:29,420
And now what we
do is we're going

978
00:46:29,420 --> 00:46:33,020
to compute those upper
confidence bounds, OK?

979
00:46:33,020 --> 00:46:36,200
So what we want to do is compute
this upper confidence bounds

980
00:46:36,200 --> 00:46:37,350
for each of the arms.

981
00:46:37,350 --> 00:46:43,190
So UCB of A1 to A3, OK?

982
00:46:43,190 --> 00:46:49,430
And so this would be 1
plus square root of 2 log

983
00:46:49,430 --> 00:46:56,000
or delta over 1, same for this
one, and then 0 plus square root

984
00:46:56,000 --> 00:46:58,045
2 log 1 over delta.

985
00:47:00,720 --> 00:47:01,220
OK.

986
00:47:01,220 --> 00:47:05,270
So in this case, you would pick
a1 or a2 with equal probability

987
00:47:05,270 --> 00:47:07,470
because the upper confidence
bound is identical.

988
00:47:11,420 --> 00:47:11,920
OK.

989
00:47:11,920 --> 00:47:13,550
So we select the arg max.

990
00:47:13,550 --> 00:47:19,510
Let's say that we pick a--

991
00:47:19,510 --> 00:47:20,010
OK?

992
00:47:20,010 --> 00:47:21,593
And now we're going
to, again, compute

993
00:47:21,593 --> 00:47:23,860
the upper confidence bound.

994
00:47:23,860 --> 00:47:25,960
So in this case,
what would happen

995
00:47:25,960 --> 00:47:29,080
is you would still have--
you'd have the following.

996
00:47:29,080 --> 00:47:35,800
You would have UCB of a1 is
equal to 1 plus square root 2

997
00:47:35,800 --> 00:47:41,530
log 1 over delta
divide by 2, UCB a2 is

998
00:47:41,530 --> 00:47:47,080
equal to 1 plus square root
2 log 1 over delta over 1,

999
00:47:47,080 --> 00:47:53,830
and UCB a3 is equal to 0
plus square root 2 log 1

1000
00:47:53,830 --> 00:47:56,830
over delta divided by 1.

1001
00:47:56,830 --> 00:48:00,610
So you can see here
is that we've now

1002
00:48:00,610 --> 00:48:03,280
reduced our upper confidence
bound, because we've

1003
00:48:03,280 --> 00:48:04,338
learned something new.

1004
00:48:04,338 --> 00:48:06,880
Now, in this case, we happen to
have also gotten high reward.

1005
00:48:06,880 --> 00:48:08,160
But either way, we
learned something new.

1006
00:48:08,160 --> 00:48:09,827
We could shrink our
confidence intervals

1007
00:48:09,827 --> 00:48:11,850
because we have
additional accounts.

1008
00:48:11,850 --> 00:48:14,190
Just to make sure I'm
understanding correctly,

1009
00:48:14,190 --> 00:48:18,240
the delta is something that
we would select to figure out

1010
00:48:18,240 --> 00:48:20,710
or to choose our
confidence bounds?

1011
00:48:20,710 --> 00:48:21,667
Yeah, great question.

1012
00:48:21,667 --> 00:48:24,000
So, yes, we haven't talked a
lot about how we set delta.

1013
00:48:24,000 --> 00:48:25,720
Going to be a couple
criteria for it.

1014
00:48:25,720 --> 00:48:29,220
In general, we're going to need
all of these confidence bounds

1015
00:48:29,220 --> 00:48:31,300
to hold for all time
steps for all arms.

1016
00:48:31,300 --> 00:48:33,300
So we're going to need
to do some union bounding

1017
00:48:33,300 --> 00:48:36,930
to make sure all of them
simultaneously hold,

1018
00:48:36,930 --> 00:48:39,300
because we want to have
it with high probability,

1019
00:48:39,300 --> 00:48:43,405
that all of these things
are valid at the same time.

1020
00:48:43,405 --> 00:48:45,780
In the simplest setting, we
know how many total decisions

1021
00:48:45,780 --> 00:48:49,170
we're making, and so we need to
use that information as well.

1022
00:48:49,170 --> 00:48:52,330
And then you can use those two
things to bound the regret,

1023
00:48:52,330 --> 00:48:54,660
as we'll see.

1024
00:48:54,660 --> 00:48:57,360
So you can see, this is why it's
a bit different than greedy.

1025
00:48:57,360 --> 00:49:00,312
Because we are still using
our empirical averages

1026
00:49:00,312 --> 00:49:01,770
but then these
confidence intervals

1027
00:49:01,770 --> 00:49:06,960
are going to change so that
over time, these will alternate

1028
00:49:06,960 --> 00:49:10,090
often, depending on what
rewards you're getting,

1029
00:49:10,090 --> 00:49:12,220
and you may
periodically take a3.

1030
00:49:12,220 --> 00:49:15,160
Because with that little data,
there is some probability

1031
00:49:15,160 --> 00:49:18,498
that a3 is just as
good as a1 and a2,

1032
00:49:18,498 --> 00:49:20,290
particularly after you
get additional data.

1033
00:49:20,290 --> 00:49:22,000
So we'll alternate
between the arms

1034
00:49:22,000 --> 00:49:23,835
based on these upper
confidence bounds.

1035
00:49:26,890 --> 00:49:29,540
OK.

1036
00:49:29,540 --> 00:49:32,880
Let's go ahead-- let's
skip through those here.

1037
00:49:32,880 --> 00:49:33,590
Let's go to here.

1038
00:49:33,590 --> 00:49:34,090
OK.

1039
00:49:34,090 --> 00:49:36,030
So this is-- we're just asking--

1040
00:49:36,030 --> 00:49:38,150
it's a little bit subtle.

1041
00:49:38,150 --> 00:49:40,028
If you have a fixed
number of time steps,

1042
00:49:40,028 --> 00:49:41,570
like, you know the
total you're going

1043
00:49:41,570 --> 00:49:47,250
to make, like, T decisions,
you can set t to be roughly--

1044
00:49:47,250 --> 00:49:51,380
you probably want
this divide by a.

1045
00:49:51,380 --> 00:49:53,640
This is because you
could use a union bound.

1046
00:49:53,640 --> 00:49:54,900
So why are we doing this?

1047
00:49:54,900 --> 00:49:57,170
We want these upper
confidence bounds to be valid,

1048
00:49:57,170 --> 00:49:59,420
and we need them to be valid
at every single time step

1049
00:49:59,420 --> 00:50:02,810
because we are using
them to make decisions.

1050
00:50:02,810 --> 00:50:06,385
So this is also related to
false discovery and other things

1051
00:50:06,385 --> 00:50:08,760
like that if you've heard
about them in machine learning.

1052
00:50:08,760 --> 00:50:09,980
So what we're going
to use here is

1053
00:50:09,980 --> 00:50:12,272
we're going to think about
all of these as being events

1054
00:50:12,272 --> 00:50:14,022
that these confidence
bounds hold,

1055
00:50:14,022 --> 00:50:15,980
and what we mean by that
is that they really do

1056
00:50:15,980 --> 00:50:18,870
contain the true value--

1057
00:50:18,870 --> 00:50:20,885
the true unknown value
with high probability.

1058
00:50:20,885 --> 00:50:22,760
So what we're going to
say is the probability

1059
00:50:22,760 --> 00:50:24,260
that all of these
events hold, which

1060
00:50:24,260 --> 00:50:26,270
means that all of our
confidence intervals

1061
00:50:26,270 --> 00:50:29,230
are valid for all of the arms,
for all of the time steps,

1062
00:50:29,230 --> 00:50:30,910
we're just going to
use a union bound,

1063
00:50:30,910 --> 00:50:32,535
which says we're just
going to sum over

1064
00:50:32,535 --> 00:50:35,410
the probability of each of
them over all of those events.

1065
00:50:35,410 --> 00:50:39,210
So that would be roughly
the number of arms times T.

1066
00:50:39,210 --> 00:50:42,390
And so that's why you can then
just divide your confidence

1067
00:50:42,390 --> 00:50:45,300
interval, your delta--

1068
00:50:45,300 --> 00:50:46,830
so you can just
divide your delta

1069
00:50:46,830 --> 00:50:49,965
into delta divided by t
times the size of your a,

1070
00:50:49,965 --> 00:50:52,440
and that generally
is sufficient.

1071
00:50:52,440 --> 00:50:53,880
And just to think
about what that

1072
00:50:53,880 --> 00:50:56,160
will do in terms of your
bounds, so remember,

1073
00:50:56,160 --> 00:50:58,930
we had a log 1 over delta term.

1074
00:50:58,930 --> 00:51:00,930
So that means you would
get something like this,

1075
00:51:00,930 --> 00:51:04,940
log t a divided by delta.

1076
00:51:07,560 --> 00:51:11,310
So generally, the union building
blows up your long term.

1077
00:51:11,310 --> 00:51:13,770
There's various
approaches, including

1078
00:51:13,770 --> 00:51:15,360
law of iterated
logarithms and others

1079
00:51:15,360 --> 00:51:18,638
to try to get this
term to be smaller.

1080
00:51:18,638 --> 00:51:20,180
So you can do tighter
things on this.

1081
00:51:23,368 --> 00:51:25,230
All right.

1082
00:51:25,230 --> 00:51:25,730
OK.

1083
00:51:25,730 --> 00:51:26,680
So let's think about--

1084
00:51:26,680 --> 00:51:28,690
I promised you that
we're going to be

1085
00:51:28,690 --> 00:51:31,650
able to use this type of
idea to get sublinear regret.

1086
00:51:31,650 --> 00:51:33,940
So let's go through
a proof sketch

1087
00:51:33,940 --> 00:51:36,790
to think about how
this actually enables

1088
00:51:36,790 --> 00:51:39,910
us to get much better
performance than what

1089
00:51:39,910 --> 00:51:41,740
we've seen before.

1090
00:51:41,740 --> 00:51:42,790
All right.

1091
00:51:42,790 --> 00:51:45,910
So what this statement says--
and I'll just put a pointer in.

1092
00:51:45,910 --> 00:51:50,630
So it's in the references
under the website,

1093
00:51:50,630 --> 00:51:52,580
but there's a great book on--

1094
00:51:52,580 --> 00:52:01,920
I think it's just called Bandit
Algorithms by Tor Lattimore

1095
00:52:01,920 --> 00:52:07,270
and Csaba Szepesárí,
which I think maybe

1096
00:52:07,270 --> 00:52:09,295
came out in 2019 or 2000.

1097
00:52:09,295 --> 00:52:11,420
I'm trying to remember,
but they have a great book.

1098
00:52:11,420 --> 00:52:13,335
So it came out of a
series of blog posts

1099
00:52:13,335 --> 00:52:14,960
they were doing on
multi-armed bandits,

1100
00:52:14,960 --> 00:52:16,750
and then they turned
it into a book.

1101
00:52:16,750 --> 00:52:18,170
And so this is a
really nice one.

1102
00:52:18,170 --> 00:52:21,850
And if you go there, I think
approximately chapter 7,

1103
00:52:21,850 --> 00:52:24,550
they're going to do a much more
rigorous version of this proof

1104
00:52:24,550 --> 00:52:26,355
compared to what
I'm doing today.

1105
00:52:26,355 --> 00:52:27,730
What I'm going to
try to do today

1106
00:52:27,730 --> 00:52:30,610
is just to give you a
flavor of types of bounds

1107
00:52:30,610 --> 00:52:33,370
that you might want to prove
in these sorts of cases

1108
00:52:33,370 --> 00:52:37,540
and how we end up
getting sublinear regret.

1109
00:52:37,540 --> 00:52:40,370
So what this result
says is the following.

1110
00:52:40,370 --> 00:52:42,430
If you think back,
what we said before is

1111
00:52:42,430 --> 00:52:47,110
we could bound the expected
regret by how many times

1112
00:52:47,110 --> 00:52:47,950
we make--

1113
00:52:47,950 --> 00:52:51,730
we choose an arm and how
much gap or loss we have

1114
00:52:51,730 --> 00:52:53,440
whenever we choose it.

1115
00:52:53,440 --> 00:52:55,270
And so one thing
that we could do

1116
00:52:55,270 --> 00:52:57,608
is then try to just
think about, well, we--

1117
00:52:57,608 --> 00:52:59,650
we don't know what the
gaps are, but the gaps, we

1118
00:52:59,650 --> 00:53:02,080
can just write down as the
difference between the expected

1119
00:53:02,080 --> 00:53:04,860
reward of that arm versus
the true reward of that arm.

1120
00:53:04,860 --> 00:53:06,540
That's not something
we can influence.

1121
00:53:06,540 --> 00:53:08,582
The thing that we can
influence is how many times

1122
00:53:08,582 --> 00:53:10,640
we're selecting bad arms.

1123
00:53:10,640 --> 00:53:13,830
So what this says is that
if an arm is suboptimal,

1124
00:53:13,830 --> 00:53:16,620
the number of times that we
pull it, number of times,

1125
00:53:16,620 --> 00:53:18,980
we take that action in
upper confidence bounds,

1126
00:53:18,980 --> 00:53:21,590
scales as a constant C prime--

1127
00:53:21,590 --> 00:53:23,130
not going to tell
you what that is.

1128
00:53:23,130 --> 00:53:24,770
Often in the algorithms,
they don't tell you

1129
00:53:24,770 --> 00:53:25,603
what that is either.

1130
00:53:25,603 --> 00:53:27,680
I mean, it'll be somewhere
in the fine print.

1131
00:53:27,680 --> 00:53:32,267
The point is that constant can't
depend on parts of the domain.

1132
00:53:32,267 --> 00:53:34,850
So it can't depend on the number
of arms or the gaps or things

1133
00:53:34,850 --> 00:53:35,870
like that.

1134
00:53:35,870 --> 00:53:38,180
It could be like
37, for example.

1135
00:53:38,180 --> 00:53:42,050
So a constant times
log of 1 over delta,

1136
00:53:42,050 --> 00:53:47,975
delta squared plus pi
squared over 3 plus 1, OK?

1137
00:53:47,975 --> 00:53:49,940
So why is this
interesting, before we

1138
00:53:49,940 --> 00:53:51,300
get into how do we prove this?

1139
00:53:51,300 --> 00:53:55,440
This is interesting because
it says if the gap is large,

1140
00:53:55,440 --> 00:53:58,640
we're going to take
it many less times.

1141
00:53:58,640 --> 00:54:01,010
So if the gap is
really small, then it

1142
00:54:01,010 --> 00:54:02,540
means that we're
going to-- we might

1143
00:54:02,540 --> 00:54:04,530
sample that action a lot more.

1144
00:54:04,530 --> 00:54:08,810
And if the gap is large, we're
going to take it less, OK?

1145
00:54:08,810 --> 00:54:12,950
And then we can combine
that with this equation.

1146
00:54:12,950 --> 00:54:16,277
And what happens
in that case is--

1147
00:54:16,277 --> 00:54:18,110
I'll go through that
part before we actually

1148
00:54:18,110 --> 00:54:19,485
think about-- so
what we're going

1149
00:54:19,485 --> 00:54:21,470
to focus on doing a
proof sketch of for today

1150
00:54:21,470 --> 00:54:23,870
is to focus on this part.

1151
00:54:23,870 --> 00:54:26,040
But let's just think,
if we could prove that,

1152
00:54:26,040 --> 00:54:27,480
why that would show the second.

1153
00:54:27,480 --> 00:54:30,890
Well, what we would get in
this case is we would say,

1154
00:54:30,890 --> 00:54:34,450
we'd get this term
plugged into here.

1155
00:54:34,450 --> 00:54:38,770
And the main thing that
would happen there is this

1156
00:54:38,770 --> 00:54:41,200
would become delta,
because we've multiplied it

1157
00:54:41,200 --> 00:54:42,940
by a delta on top.

1158
00:54:42,940 --> 00:54:46,510
And then here, if you
assume that everything

1159
00:54:46,510 --> 00:54:52,250
is bounded between 0 and 1, then
the deltas are at most 1, 2.

1160
00:54:52,250 --> 00:54:55,570
So you can get-- this is just
the number of actions times 1

1161
00:54:55,570 --> 00:54:59,290
plus pi squared
over 3, this term.

1162
00:54:59,290 --> 00:55:02,680
So this just shows what
your total regret would be,

1163
00:55:02,680 --> 00:55:05,383
in this case, your
total expected regret.

1164
00:55:05,383 --> 00:55:07,300
As I said, there's quite
a bit more subtleties

1165
00:55:07,300 --> 00:55:10,440
to the formal proof, but this
just gives sort of a rough idea.

1166
00:55:10,440 --> 00:55:12,190
So we have any questions
on that before we

1167
00:55:12,190 --> 00:55:14,230
dig into how we show
the first part, which

1168
00:55:14,230 --> 00:55:17,210
is the total number of times
we're going to take arms,

1169
00:55:17,210 --> 00:55:20,470
we're going to pull a
particular arm, scales with 1

1170
00:55:20,470 --> 00:55:22,170
over the size of
the gap squared.

1171
00:55:30,260 --> 00:55:30,760
All right.

1172
00:55:30,760 --> 00:55:31,593
Let's go through it.

1173
00:55:31,593 --> 00:55:36,000
So this is going to heavily
rely on the Hoeffding

1174
00:55:36,000 --> 00:55:38,110
inequality and the
upper confidence bounds.

1175
00:55:38,110 --> 00:55:43,140
So remember what we
saw before is let's

1176
00:55:43,140 --> 00:55:44,590
imagine that we've got this.

1177
00:55:44,590 --> 00:55:48,070
So we're going to say, this
was our upper confidence bound.

1178
00:55:48,070 --> 00:55:50,550
So we had this upper
confidence bound.

1179
00:55:50,550 --> 00:55:58,480
And again, I'm going to
be loose with the deltas.

1180
00:55:58,480 --> 00:55:58,980
OK.

1181
00:55:58,980 --> 00:56:01,420
We'd have to be a little bit
more formal about it in general,

1182
00:56:01,420 --> 00:56:02,378
but let's look at this.

1183
00:56:02,378 --> 00:56:07,710
So this is going to
be the true value,

1184
00:56:07,710 --> 00:56:13,770
and this is our
empirical estimate, OK?

1185
00:56:13,770 --> 00:56:17,010
So what Hoeffding inequality
had told us is to say,

1186
00:56:17,010 --> 00:56:18,990
the difference between
the true expected value

1187
00:56:18,990 --> 00:56:21,780
for an arm and your
empirical average

1188
00:56:21,780 --> 00:56:27,630
is greater than this quantity,
our upper confidence bound,

1189
00:56:27,630 --> 00:56:33,040
with probability no more
than 1 delta over T, OK?

1190
00:56:33,040 --> 00:56:35,120
So now let's think
about the following.

1191
00:56:35,120 --> 00:56:41,620
Let's think about
the times we pull

1192
00:56:41,620 --> 00:56:47,380
a, which is not equal to
a star, and delta a, which

1193
00:56:47,380 --> 00:56:49,450
is not equal to 0.

1194
00:56:49,450 --> 00:56:52,310
These are the only things we
care about in terms of regret.

1195
00:56:52,310 --> 00:56:54,770
If we're pulling a
star, we have 0 regret.

1196
00:56:54,770 --> 00:56:59,135
If we are pulling an arm
that has delta a equals 0,

1197
00:56:59,135 --> 00:57:01,510
that also means that it has
zero regret, because it means

1198
00:57:01,510 --> 00:57:03,280
it's tied with an optimal arm.

1199
00:57:03,280 --> 00:57:05,320
So the only things that
we care about bounding

1200
00:57:05,320 --> 00:57:08,320
here is to think about
for that Nt of a,

1201
00:57:08,320 --> 00:57:12,900
how many times are we pulling
arms that are not optimal, OK?

1202
00:57:12,900 --> 00:57:13,400
All right.

1203
00:57:13,400 --> 00:57:16,160
So what we're going to
do is observed a couple.

1204
00:57:16,160 --> 00:57:21,290
So if the confidence
interval holds,

1205
00:57:21,290 --> 00:57:23,740
so we can think
of if this holds,

1206
00:57:23,740 --> 00:57:25,160
then we have the following.

1207
00:57:25,160 --> 00:57:34,050
We have the Q(a) minus C log
over delta divided by Nt(a).

1208
00:57:34,050 --> 00:57:43,570
So Here I'll say,
if one holds, which

1209
00:57:43,570 --> 00:57:48,820
is less than or equal to Qt
hat of a, which is less than

1210
00:57:48,820 --> 00:57:53,740
or equal to Q of a plus
square root C log 1

1211
00:57:53,740 --> 00:57:57,640
over delta divided by Nt of a.

1212
00:57:57,640 --> 00:57:59,892
This just says, if your
confidence intervals holds,

1213
00:57:59,892 --> 00:58:02,350
what it means for it to hold
is that confidence interval is

1214
00:58:02,350 --> 00:58:05,320
wide enough that it
contains your true value.

1215
00:58:05,320 --> 00:58:07,550
And the upper confidence
part is higher than that,

1216
00:58:07,550 --> 00:58:10,092
and the lower confidence bound
is lower than your true value.

1217
00:58:10,092 --> 00:58:14,200
So this is just holds if our
confidence intervals hold, OK?

1218
00:58:14,200 --> 00:58:19,690
Now, if we pull a
instead of a star--

1219
00:58:19,690 --> 00:58:29,040
so under UCB algorithm,
we have the following.

1220
00:58:29,040 --> 00:58:34,290
We know that the upper
confidence bound of a

1221
00:58:34,290 --> 00:58:36,905
was greater than the upper--

1222
00:58:42,320 --> 00:58:44,700
because that's why you pick
this alternative action.

1223
00:58:44,700 --> 00:58:48,417
So in this case, if we
pull this arm a, that

1224
00:58:48,417 --> 00:58:50,000
means that it's upper
confidence bound

1225
00:58:50,000 --> 00:58:51,890
was different than the
upper confidence bound

1226
00:58:51,890 --> 00:58:54,960
of the optimal action,
and it was more preferred.

1227
00:58:54,960 --> 00:58:57,230
So that's the only time we
ever take the wrong action,

1228
00:58:57,230 --> 00:58:58,730
is if it's upper
confidence bound is

1229
00:58:58,730 --> 00:59:00,650
higher than the other actions.

1230
00:59:00,650 --> 00:59:01,262
OK.

1231
00:59:01,262 --> 00:59:02,720
So let's write down
what that means

1232
00:59:02,720 --> 00:59:04,560
in terms of its upper bounds.

1233
00:59:04,560 --> 00:59:06,860
So the definition
of upper bounds

1234
00:59:06,860 --> 00:59:12,770
here is that Qt of a
plus square root C log 1

1235
00:59:12,770 --> 00:59:21,080
over delta divided by Nt of a
is greater than Qt of a star

1236
00:59:21,080 --> 00:59:31,370
plus C log 1 over delta
divided by Nt of a star, OK?

1237
00:59:31,370 --> 00:59:33,290
Because that's just the
definition of our two

1238
00:59:33,290 --> 00:59:34,350
